{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<h1>Legal</h1>
<p class="hint">Every community has these two whether or not anybody has thought about them, so this
  server ships a considered version of each and publishes it from the first day. They say plainly
  that the people who wrote this software and whoever provides the machine are not parties to
  anything that happens here &mdash; which in a self-hosted community is the fact somebody has to
  be told.</p>
<p class="problem">These are a starting point written by people who are not lawyers, and they cannot
  know which country you are in. Read them. If this community carries real risk &mdash; money,
  children, medical advice, an organisation with something to lose &mdash; have somebody qualified
  read them too.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>document</th><th>state</th><th>address</th><th class="actions"></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {{#docs}}
  <tr>
    <td><strong>{{title}}</strong><div class="dim">{{summary}}</div></td>
    <td>{{#overridden}}<span class="pill blue">yours</span>{{/overridden}}{{^overridden}}<span class="pill">as shipped</span>{{/overridden}}
      {{#overridden}}<div class="dim">{{updated}}</div>{{/overridden}}</td>
    <td class="mono"><a href="{{publicUrl}}">{{publicUrl}}</a></td>
    <td class="actions"><a href="{{editUrl}}">Edit</a></td>
  </tr>
  {{/docs}}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Two things they assume</h2>
<ul class="cando">
  <li><span class="dim">everywhere</span> every email this community sends links to the terms and says that using the community accepts them</li>
  <li><span class="dim">open</span> both pages are readable without signing in, because most of the people receiving that link do not have an account yet</li>
</ul>
<p class="hint">Inside the text, <code>{{lbrace}}community{{rbrace}}</code> becomes this community's
  name and <code>{{lbrace}}domain{{rbrace}}</code> becomes its address, so a document written once is
  right after somebody renames the community.</p>
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
